  "d53544264ee015b963a8a28e0b2b4662231e582dc7dcc394b655785ae722df94": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. A Bartholin gland cyst was initially considered but excluded because of the patient’s age and absence of pain or discomfort with activity — “The cyst was excluded, because it is more common in young women, and causes symptoms of pain during walking, sitting, and intercourse, which were absent in this case.”\n2. Chondroid syringoma was diagnosed three times due to the lesion’s slow growth, encapsulation, and painless, solid features — “CS — which is also known as pleomorphic adenoma or benign mixed tumor — is a rare encapsulated, slow-growing, well-defined, painless and solid tumor.”\n3. Clear cell hidradenoma was considered after the second excision because of the lobular architecture of cuboidal cells without atypia — “lobular glandular proliferation of cuboid cells with no anaplasia.”\n4. Adenoid cystic carcinoma became the leading diagnosis when histology showed a characteristic cribriform architecture with nests of basaloid cells amid hyalinized stroma — “The cribriform pattern is the most typical characterization, which is formed by nests of tumor cells interrupted by basophilic basement membrane materials.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. The patient has a history of recurrent masses in the labia majora, with previous histopathology showing chondroid syringoma, and the most recent excised mass was also reported as chondroid syringoma.\n2. The patient had a previous histology showing clear cell hidradenoma, which is a type of benign tumor that can recur, and the current clinical presentation is similar to the previous recurrence.\n\nAnalysis of Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 1: \"A Bartholin gland cyst was initially considered but excluded because of the patient’s age and absence of pain or discomfort with activity — “The cyst was excluded, because it is more common in young women, and causes symptoms of pain during walking, sitting, and intercourse, which were absent in this case.”\"