  "4daf47467d0ff481c341ffac327cf1481631b3e3fb5cad78fe4ab3e578e2f20d": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Consideration of intraligamentary myoma with cystic degeneration — “suspected to originate from intraligamentery myoma with cystic degeneration…”\n2. Consideration of left ovarian fibroid — “differential diagnosis of left ovarian fibroid.”\n3. Consideration of infected hematometra — “hypoechoic mass in the uterine cavity, suspected of infected hematometra.”\n4. Consideration of tubo-ovarian abscess — “CT scan revealed a cystic multilocular left adnexa lesion with thick septa and solid intra-lesion component suspected of tubo-ovarian abscess.”\n5. Consideration of benign adnexal masses — “Benign adnexal masses … are gynecological cases resulting in chronic pelvic mass.”\n6. Consideration of endometriosis — “Symptoms may mimic endometriosis in the pelvis.”\n7. Consideration of primary ovarian neoplasms — “primary ovarian neoplasms are gynecological cases resulting in chronic pelvic mass.”\n8. Consideration of colorectal masses — “Colorectal masses … are non-gynecologic causes.”\n9. Consideration of hematoma — “Colorectal masses, hematoma, and soft tissue tumors are non-gynecologic causes.”\n10. Consideration of soft tissue tumors — “Colorectal masses, hematoma, and soft tissue tumors are non-gynecologic causes.”\n11. Consideration of teratoma — “Teratoma and schwannoma can be differentiated by tumor markers Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) and CA-125, whereas schwannoma is suspected if these tumor markers are negative.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\nDiagnosis: Leiomyoma with cystic degeneration\nEvidence: The patient's history of progressive abdominal enlargement, difficulty with defecation and voiding, and the presence of a smooth, firm pelvic mass on rectovaginal examination, along with imaging findings of a large hypoechoic mass within the uterine cavity and a separate solid mass in the left adnexa, are consistent with a large uterine leiomyoma with cystic degeneration.\nDiagnosis: Infected hemato