  "bc91d6b4e651a2e2435a3799bcaa2ca4cec422030735438a60b39c491d5dd96b": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Gastric outlet obstruction was considered given the patient’s intractable vomiting and abdominal distension — “…differential diagnoses were considered given the above presentation: • Gastric outlet obstruction.”\n2. Peptic stricture of the stomach was considered as a cause of outlet narrowing — “…differential diagnoses were considered given the above presentation: • Peptic stricture of the stomach.”\n3. Neoplasm of the stomach was considered because tumors can obstruct gastric outflow — “…differential diagnoses were considered given the above presentation: • Neoplasm of the stomach.”\n4. Annular pancreas, diabetic gastroparesis, internal hernia, and duodenal bezoar were considered as alternative causes of obstruction but were excluded by endoscopy — “An upper gastrointestinal endoscopy was also done to rule out other causes and it was found to be normal.”\n5. Peptic ulcer disease and duodenal tumors were also considered among other causes of obstruction and were excluded by imaging and endoscopy — “…other causes of duodenal obstruction like peptic ulcer disease and duodenal tumors.”\n6. Scleroderma and other collagen or vascular disorders were considered as mimics of the presentation — “…conditions that closely mimic SMA syndrome ranging from scleroderma to other collagen and vascular disorders…”\n7. Vascular compression due to a reduced aortomesenteric angle was supported by CT findings demonstrating duodenal compression — “CT scans were used to rule in for duodenal obstruction due to a reduced aortomesenteric angle in the patient.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\nDiagnosis: Superior Mesenteric Artery Syndrome\nEvidence: The patient's symptoms of intractable vomiting, postprandial upper abdominal pain, and bloating, combined with the finding of compression of the third portion of the duodenum between the superior mesenteric artery and the aorta, are consistent with this diagnosis.\nDiagnosis: Gastric Outlet Obstruction\nEvidence: The patient's symptoms of vomiting, abdominal pain, and bloating, along with the finding of marked gastric distension, could also suggest a gastric outlet obstruction, although the lack of intrinsic luminal lesions on upper gastrointestinal endoscopy makes this less likely.\n\nAnalysis of potential matches:\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 1: Gastric outlet obstruction was considered given the patient’s intractable vomiting and abdominal distension — “…differential diagnoses were considered given the above presentation: • Gastric outlet obstruction.”\n- Potential match from Predicted Diagnostic Reasons: \"The patient's symptoms of vomiting, abdominal pain, and bloating, along with the finding of marked gastric distension, could also suggest a gastric outlet obstruction, although the lack of intrinsic luminal lesions on upper gastrointestinal endoscopy makes this less likely.\"\n    - Pros: This statement explicitly mentions \"gastric outlet obstruction\" and links it to symptoms like \"vomiting\" and \"gastric distension,\" which aligns with the groundtruth's \"intractable vomiting and abdominal distension.\" It also states that it \"could also suggest\" this diagnosis, indicating it was considered.\n    - Cons: The groundtruth statement is more direct about it being a differential diagnosis, while the predicted statement frames it as a possibility that is then made less likely. However, the core idea of considering GOO based on symptoms is present.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 2: Peptic stricture of the stomach was considered as a cause of outlet narrowing — “…differential diagnoses were considered given the above presentation: • Peptic stricture of the stomach.”\n- No direct match in Predicted Diagnostic Reasons.\n    - Pros: None.\n    - Cons: The predicted reasons do not mention \"peptic stricture of the stomach\" or any specific cause of outlet narrowing other than SMA syndrome itself.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 3: Neoplasm of the stomach was considered because tumors can obstruct gastric outflow — “…differential diagnoses were considered given the above presentation: • Neoplasm of the stomach.”\n- No direct match in Predicted Diagnostic Reasons.\n    - Pros: None.\n    - Cons: The predicted reasons do not mention \"neoplasm of the stomach\" or any other specific tumor as a differential.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 4: Annular pancreas, diabetic gastroparesis, internal hernia, and duodenal bezoar were considered as alternative causes of obstruction but were excluded by endoscopy — “An upper gastrointestinal endoscopy was also done to rule out other causes and it was found to be normal.”\n- Potential match from Predicted Diagnostic Reasons: \"The patient's symptoms of vomiting, abdominal pain, and bloating, along with the finding of marked gastric distension, could also suggest a gastric outlet obstruction, although the lack of intrinsic luminal lesions on upper gastrointestinal endoscopy makes this less likely.\"\n    - Pros: This statement mentions \"lack of intrinsic luminal lesions on upper gastrointestinal endoscopy,\" which implies that endoscopy was used to rule out certain causes of obstruction. This aligns with the groundtruth's mention of endoscopy ruling out other causes.\n    - Cons: The predicted statement does not list the specific conditions (annular pancreas, diabetic gastroparesis, internal hernia, duodenal bezoar) mentioned in the groundtruth. It only generally refers to \"intrinsic luminal lesions.\"\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 5: Peptic ulcer disease and duodenal tumors were also considered among other causes of obstruction and were excluded by imaging and endoscopy — “…other causes of duodenal obstruction like peptic ulcer disease and duodenal tumors.”\n- Potential match from Predicted Diagnostic Reasons: \"The patient's symptoms of vomiting, abdominal pain, and bloating, along with the finding of marked gastric distension, could also suggest a gastric outlet obstruction, although the lack of intrinsic luminal lesions on upper gastrointestinal endoscopy makes this less likely.\"\n    - Pros: Similar to GR4, this statement mentions the \"lack of intrinsic luminal lesions on upper gastrointestinal endoscopy,\" which would exclude conditions like peptic ulcer disease and duodenal tumors if they were intrinsic luminal lesions.\n    - Cons: The predicted statement does not explicitly mention \"peptic ulcer disease\" or \"duodenal tumors.\" It's a general statement about endoscopy findings.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 6: Scleroderma and other collagen or vascular disorders were considered as mimics of the presentation — “…conditions that closely mimic SMA syndrome ranging from scleroderma to other collagen and vascular disorders…”\n- No direct match in Predicted Diagnostic Reasons.\n    - Pros: None.\n    - Cons: The predicted reasons do not mention scleroderma or other collagen/vascular disorders.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 7: Vascular compression due to a reduced aortomesenteric angle was supported by CT findings demonstrating duodenal compression — “CT scans were used to rule in for duodenal obstruction due to a reduced aortomesenteric angle in the patient.”\n- Potential match from Predicted Diagnostic Reasons: \"The patient's symptoms of intractable vomiting, postprandial upper abdominal pain, and bloating, combined with the finding of compression of the third portion of the duodenum between the superior mesenteric artery and the aorta, are consistent with this diagnosis.\"\n    - Pros: This statement directly mentions \"compression of the third portion of the duodenum between the superior mesenteric artery and the aorta,\" which is the anatomical basis for vascular compression due to a reduced aortomesenteric angle. While it doesn't explicitly state \"CT findings,\" this anatomical finding is typically confirmed by imaging like CT. It also links this finding to the diagnosis of SMA syndrome, which is the outcome of such compression.\n    - Cons: It doesn't explicitly state \"CT findings\" or \"reduced aortomesenteric angle\" verbatim, but the description of the compression is highly indicative of the groundtruth.\n\nSummary of final matching decisions:\n- Groundtruth 1 matches with the predicted statement about gastric outlet obstruction.\n- Groundtruth 2 has no match.\n- Groundtruth 3 has no match.\n- Groundtruth 4 matches with the predicted statement about the lack of intrinsic luminal lesions on endoscopy, as it implies ruling out other causes.\n- Groundtruth 5 matches with the predicted statement about the lack of intrinsic luminal lesions on endoscopy, as it implies ruling out other causes like peptic ulcer disease and duodenal tumors.\n- Groundtruth 6 has no match.\n- Groundtruth 7 matches with the predicted statement describing the compression of the duodenum between the SMA and aorta, which is the direct consequence of a reduced aortomesenteric angle and is typically confirmed by CT.\n\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [\n      \"The patient's symptoms of vomiting, abdominal pain, and bloating, along with the finding of marked gastric distension, could also suggest a gastric outlet obstruction, although the lack of intrinsic luminal lesions on upper gastrointestinal endoscopy makes this less likely.\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [],\n    \"4\": [\n      \"The patient's symptoms of vomiting, abdominal pain, and bloating, along with the finding of marked gastric distension, could also suggest a gastric outlet obstruction, although the lack of intrinsic luminal lesions on upper gastrointestinal endoscopy makes this less likely.\"\n    ],\n    \"5\": [\n      \"The patient's symptoms of vomiting, abdominal pain, and bloating, along with the finding of marked gastric distension, could also suggest a gastric outlet obstruction, although the lack of intrinsic luminal lesions on upper gastrointestinal endoscopy makes this less likely.\"\n    ],\n    \"6\": [],\n    \"7\": [\n      \"The patient's symptoms of intractable vomiting, postprandial upper abdominal pain, and bloating, combined with the finding of compression of the third portion of the duodenum between the superior mesenteric artery and the aorta, are consistent with this diagnosis.\"\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```",
