    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Liver adenoma was initially considered given prior biopsies—“The primary differential diagnosis was bleeding episode of liver adenoma—misled by the previously obtained histopathology report.”\n2. Hepatocellular carcinoma was suspected because of persistent hypervascular lesions with necrosis—“Because of a suspected atypical hepatocellular carcinoma, an additional magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) study was performed … and a surgical resection was indicated.”\n3. Hepatic-origin tumors were excluded by immunohistochemistry negative for liver markers—“Immunohistochemical staining revealed … and lack of reaction against … Arginase-1, for exclusion of a liver-own tumor.”\n4. Metastatic liver tumor and intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma were considered in adults with hypervascular hepatic masses—“In adults its differential diagnoses can be metastatic liver tumor or intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma.”\n5. Liver hematoma was part of the differential given imaging evidence of hemorrhage—“Preoperative CT-scan … showing hypervascularized hepatic lesions with central necrosis and associated hematoma.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. The patient has a history of histologically proven adenoma without malignancy and presents with acute-onset abdominal pain and anemia due to bleeding from one of the hypervascular lesions.\n2. The patient's lesions have atypical features, such as central necrosis, which raises suspicion for an atypical hepatocellular carcinoma, prompting an elective surgical resection.\n\nAnalysis of Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 1: \"Liver adenoma was initially considered given prior biopsies—“The primary differential diagnosis was bleeding episode of liver adenoma—misled by the previously obtained histopathology report.”\"\n   - Potential match 1: \"The patient has a history of histologically proven adenoma without malignancy and presents with acute-onset abdominal pain and anemia due to blee