  "b564215f1f7af753b87e33d7c8ceefbbc4cdcb1584d34b19b29ea366cdc88ddd": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Considered post-irradiation angiosarcoma as the most common PIS subtype — “angiosarcoma accounted for 56.8 % of the lesions…”\n2. Considered malignant fibrous histiocytoma given its relative PIS frequency — “malignant fibrous histiocytoma (MFH) accounting for 15.9 %…”\n3. Considered fibrosarcoma and chondrosarcoma among PIS subtypes — “fibrosarcoma and chondrosarcoma accounting for 6.8 % of the lesions…”\n4. Considered spindle-cell phyllodes tumor in the differential of breast spindle lesions — “The differential diagnosis of leiomyosarcoma includes various types of sarcoma, such as fibrosarcoma, MFH, and phyllodes tumor.”\n5. Considered primary leiomyosarcoma of the breast but noted extreme rarity versus post-irradiation origin given latency and field — “the lesion in our patient may have been primary leiomyosarcoma, and leiomyosarcoma of the breast is extremely rare.”\n6. Confirmed leiomyosarcoma based on smooth-muscle immunophenotype — “tumor cells were positive for α-SMA, desmin, and vimentin.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\nDiagnosis: Breast Cancer Recurrence\nEvidence: The patient has a history of breast cancer and has undergone breast-conserving surgery, radiation, and endocrine therapy. The new lump in the lower outer quadrant of the left breast, which is fixed to the pectoralis major muscle and has increased in size on follow-up, suggests a possible recurrence of breast cancer.\nDiagnosis: Sarcoma\nEvidence: The patient has a history of radiation therapy to the left breast, which increases the risk of developing a sarcoma. The firm mass fixed to the pectoralis major muscle and the lack of involvement of the overlying skin and axillary lymph nodes could be consistent with a sarcoma.\n\nAnalysis of potential matches:\n\nGroundtruth 1: Considered post-irradiation angiosarcoma as the most common PIS subtype — “angiosarcoma accounted for 56.8 % of the lesions…”\n- Predicted \"The patient has a history of radiation therapy to the left breast, which increases the risk of developing a sarcoma.\":\n    - Pros: Mentions radiation therapy and increased risk of sarcoma, which is a prerequisite for considering post-irradiation angiosarcoma.\n    - Cons: Does not specifically mention angiosarcoma or its prevalence. It's a general statement about sarcoma risk.\n\nGroundtruth 2: Considered malignant fibrous histiocytoma given its relative PIS frequency — “malignant fibrous histiocytoma (MFH) accounting for 15.9 %…”\n- No direct match. The predicted reasons mention \"sarcoma\" generally but do not specify MFH.\n\nGroundtruth 3: Considered fibrosarcoma and chondrosarcoma among PIS subtypes — “fibrosarcoma and chondrosarcoma accounting for 6.8 % of the lesions…”\n- No direct match. The predicted reasons mention \"sarcoma\" generally but do not specify fibrosarcoma or chondrosarcoma.\n\nGroundtruth 4: Considered spindle-cell phyllodes tumor in the differential of breast spindle lesions — “The differential diagnosis of leiomyosarcoma includes various types of sarcoma, such as fibrosarcoma, MFH, and phyllodes tumor.”\n- No direct match. The predicted reasons mention \"sarcoma\" generally but do not specify phyllodes tumor.\n\nGroundtruth 5: Considered primary leiomyosarcoma of the breast but noted extreme rarity versus post-irradiation origin given latency and field — “the lesion in our patient may have been primary leiomyosarcoma, and leiomyosarcoma of the breast is extremely rare.”\n- Predicted \"The patient has a history of radiation therapy to the left breast, which increases the risk of developing a sarcoma.\":\n    - Pros: This statement supports the idea of a post-irradiation origin for a sarcoma, which is contrasted with the rarity of primary leiomyosarcoma in the ground truth. It provides context for why a post-irradiation sarcoma (like leiomyosarcoma) would be considered over a primary one.\n    - Cons: Does not explicitly mention leiomyosarcoma or its rarity.\n\nGroundtruth 6: Confirmed leiomyosarcoma based on smooth-muscle immunophenotype — “tumor cells were positive for α-SMA, desmin, and vimentin.”\n- No direct match. The predicted reasons do not discuss specific immunophenotypes or confirmation of a specific sarcoma type.\n\nSummary of final matching decisions:\n- Groundtruth 1: \"Considered post-irradiation angiosarcoma as the most common PIS subtype — “angiosarcoma accounted for 56.8 % of the lesions…”\" matches with \"The patient has a history of radiation therapy to the left breast, which increases the risk of developing a sarcoma.\" because the predicted statement establishes the general risk of post-radiation sarcoma, which is a prerequisite for considering specific post-irradiation sarcoma subtypes like angiosarcoma.\n- Groundtruth 2: No match.\n- Groundtruth 3: No match.\n- Groundtruth 4: No match.\n- Groundtruth 5: \"Considered primary leiomyosarcoma of the breast but noted extreme rarity versus post-irradiation origin given latency and field — “the lesion in our patient may have been primary leiomyosarcoma, and leiomyosarcoma of the breast is extremely rare.”\" matches with \"The patient has a history of radiation therapy to the left breast, which increases the risk of developing a sarcoma.\" because the predicted statement highlights the increased risk of sarcoma due to radiation, which is a key factor in differentiating a post-irradiation sarcoma from a rare primary one, as discussed in the ground truth.\n- Groundtruth 6: No match.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [\n      \"The patient has a history of radiation therapy to the left breast, which increases the risk of developing a sarcoma.\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [],\n    \"4\": [],\n    \"5\": [\n      \"The patient has a history of radiation therapy to the left breast, which increases the risk of developing a sarcoma.\"\n    ],\n    \"6\": []\n  }\n}\n```\n</diagnostic_comparison>",
