nostic Reason 2: Pocket infection was considered but excluded on imaging and clinical grounds — “haematoma or infection is excluded.”\n- Potential match from Predicted Diagnostic Reasons: None.\n    - Pros: No statement in the predicted reasons discusses the exclusion of infection.\n    - Cons: None.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 3: Cardiac device–related infective endocarditis was deemed unlikely given normal vital signs and absence of systemic inflammation — “He had normal blood pressure, normal heart rate, no signs of cardiac decompensation or cachexia.”\n- Potential match from Predicted Diagnostic Reasons: None.\n    - Pros: No statement in the predicted reasons discusses the exclusion of infective endocarditis or the patient's vital signs.\n    - Cons: None.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 4: A malignant process (primary tumour or metastasis) was considered due to the solid, vascular appearance of the mass and the patient’s oncologic history — “Presentation of a primary tumour or a metastasis in a pacemaker pocket is rare, yet several case reports are available.”\n- Potential match from Predicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n    - \"The patient has a history of multiple cancers and presents with a soft tissue mass surrounding the pacemaker site, which suggests a possible tumor recurrence or metastasis.\"\n        - Pros: This statement directly links the patient's history of multiple cancers and the soft tissue mass to the suggestion of \"tumor recurrence or metastasis,\" which aligns perfectly with \"A malignant process (primary tumour or metastasis) was considered... and the patient’s oncologic history.\"\n        - Cons: None.\n    - \"The patient's history of radiation exposure from previous cancer treatments and the presence of a soft tissue mass around the pacemaker site could suggest a possible sarcoma, which is a rare but possible complication of radiation therapy.\"\n        - Pros: This statement also points to a malignant process (sarcoma) and links it to the patient's history (radiation exposure from previous cancer treatments) and the soft tissue mass. Sarcoma is a type of tumor, fitting within the broader category of a \"malignant process.\"\n        - Cons: While it suggests a malignant process, it is more specific (sarcoma and radiation exposure) than the general \"primary tumour or metastasis\" and \"oncologic history\" in the ground truth. However, it still supports the idea of a malignant process being considered due to the patient's history and the mass.\n\nSummary of final matching decisions:\n- Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 1 has no match.\n- Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 2 has no match.\n- Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 3 has no match.\n- Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 4 matches with \"The patient has a history of multiple cancers and presents with a soft tissue mass surrounding the pacemaker site, which suggests a possible tumor recurrence or metastasis.\" and \"The patient's history of radiation exposure from previous cancer treatments and the presence of a soft tissue mass around the pacemaker site could suggest a possible sarcoma, which is a rare but possible complication of radiation therapy.\"\n\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [],\n    \"4\": [\n      \"The patient has a history of multiple cancers and presents with a soft tissue mass surrounding the pacemaker site, which suggests a possible tumor recurrence or metastasis.\",\n      \"The patient's history of radiation exposure from previous cancer treatments and the presence of a soft tissue mass around the pacemaker site could suggest a possible sarcoma, which is a rare but possible complication of radiation therapy.\"\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```",
