sting were negative.”\n- Potential match 1 (Predicted Statement 1): \"The patient's symptoms and laboratory results, including non-caseating microgranulomas in biopsies and elevated angiotensin-converting enzyme level, are consistent with sarcoidosis.\"\n    - Pros: None. This statement is about sarcoidosis.\n    - Cons: No mention of Tropheryma whipplei or ruling out Whipple's disease.\n- Potential match 2 (Predicted Statement 2): \"The patient's gastrointestinal symptoms, such as abdominal pain and diarrhea, and the presence of erythematous mucosa and congestion in the colon and terminal ileum, could also suggest Crohn's disease.\"\n    - Pros: None. This statement is about Crohn's disease.\n    - Cons: No mention of Tropheryma whipplei or ruling out Whipple's disease.\n\nGroundtruth Statement 5: Syphilis — “Rapid plasma reagin … was negative.”\n- Potential match 1 (Predicted Statement 1): \"The patient's symptoms and laboratory results, including non-caseating microgranulomas in biopsies and elevated angiotensin-converting enzyme level, are consistent with sarcoidosis.\"\n    - Pros: None. This statement is about sarcoidosis.\n    - Cons: No mention of rapid plasma reagin or ruling out syphilis.\n- Potential match 2 (Predicted Statement 2): \"The patient's gastrointestinal symptoms, such as abdominal pain and diarrhea, and the presence of erythematous mucosa and congestion in the colon and terminal ileum, could also suggest Crohn's disease.\"\n    - Pros: None. This statement is about Crohn's disease.\n    - Cons: No mention of rapid plasma reagin or ruling out syphilis.\n\nGroundtruth Statement 6: Helicobacter pylori infection — “No Helicobacter pylori organisms were identified.”\n- Potential match 1 (Predicted Statement 1): \"The patient's symptoms and laboratory results, including non-caseating microgranulomas in biopsies and elevated angiotensin-converting enzyme level, are consistent with sarcoidosis.\"\n    - Pros: None. This statement is about sarcoidosis.