    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Classical trigeminal neuralgia — “We excluded the possibility of it being a classical TN based on the lack of evidence of neurovascular conflict in MRA.”\n2. Secondary trigeminal neuralgia — “Secondary TN was excluded based on absence of space-occupying tumours, a demyelinating process or other disorders.”\n3. Cluster headache — “Despite superficial similarities…based on different localizations, duration of the attacks and accompanying autonomic signs…pain tends to migrate from one side of the face to the other, while it consistently remains limited to a single side in TN…”\n4. Persistent idiopathic facial pain — “Triggering factors other than those typically found in TN and different pain quality may indicate persistent idiopathic facial pain.”\n5. Painful post-traumatic trigeminal neuropathy — “Painful post-traumatic trigeminal neuropathy…is always preceded by a major traumatic injury and displays clear neurological abnormalities visible on neuroimaging.”\n6. Herpes zoster–related neuropathy — “Herpes zoster neuropathy…is differentiated by the presence of highly distinctive skin lesions, usually in the 1st branch division of CN V.”\n7. Glossopharyngeal neuralgia and dental pathology — “Diagnosis relies on highly specific clinical features, allowing it to be easily distinguished from aforementioned ailments.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. The patient's symptoms of episodic sharp, electric-shock–like pain in the V2 and V3 distributions of the left trigeminal nerve, triggered by jaw movement, are characteristic of trigeminal neuralgia.\n2. The onset of symptoms after receiving the COVID-19 vaccine suggests a possible link, although the exact relationship is unclear.\n\nAnalysis of potential matches:\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 1: Classical trigeminal neuralgia — “We excluded the possibility of it being a classical TN based on the lack of evidence of neurovascular conflict in MRA.”\n