ons: The groundtruth reason is about *excluding* a specific condition based on *history of trauma and imaging findings*. The predicted reason is about *supporting* TN based on symptoms. They are not equivalent.\n- Predicted Diagnostic Reason 2: \"The onset of symptoms after receiving the COVID-19 vaccine suggests a possible link, although the exact relationship is unclear.\"\n    - Pros: None.\n    - Cons: No relation to post-traumatic neuropathy.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 6: Herpes zoster–related neuropathy — “Herpes zoster neuropathy…is differentiated by the presence of highly distinctive skin lesions, usually in the 1st branch division of CN V.”\n- This statement is about *excluding* herpes zoster-related neuropathy based on the absence of distinctive skin lesions.\n- Predicted Diagnostic Reason 1: \"The patient's symptoms of episodic sharp, electric-shock–like pain in the V2 and V3 distributions of the left trigeminal nerve, triggered by jaw movement, are characteristic of trigeminal neuralgia.\"\n    - Pros: Both relate to trigeminal nerve issues.\n    - Cons: The groundtruth reason is about *excluding* a specific condition based on *absence of skin lesions*. The predicted reason is about *supporting* TN based on symptoms. They are not equivalent.\n- Predicted Diagnostic Reason 2: \"The onset of symptoms after receiving the COVID-19 vaccine suggests a possible link, although the exact relationship is unclear.\"\n    - Pros: None.\n    - Cons: No relation to herpes zoster or skin lesions.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 7: Glossopharyngeal neuralgia and dental pathology — “Diagnosis relies on highly specific clinical features, allowing it to be easily distinguished from aforementioned ailments.”\n- This statement is about *excluding* glossopharyngeal neuralgia and dental pathology based on their highly specific clinical features, which are different from TN.\n- Predicted Diagnostic Reason 1: \"The patient's symptoms of episodic sharp, electric-shock–like