  "103cabb117766ab286459d79e517fd1a9ae777376f34a4c510ee35ebcfabb189": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\n### Groundtruth Diagnostic Reasons (GDR):\n1. \"Considered meningioma because the lesion was dural-based with bone invasion and vascular supply by the middle meningeal artery — “Thus, a clinical diagnosis of osteoblastic meningioma was made.”\"\n2. \"Considered primary lymphoma as part of the differential for a dural mass — “The differential diagnosis included primary lymphoma” — but histology revealed spindle cell rather than lymphoid proliferation.\"\n3. \"Considered metastasis given the skull-invading mass — “The differential diagnosis included ... metastasis” — but staging CT and PET scans showed no extracranial involvement: “Staging CT scan of the lung and abdomen and whole body positron emission tomography (PET) scan were negative for extra cranial involvement.”\"\n4. \"Considered sarcoma due to spindle cell morphology — “The differential diagnosis included ... sarcoma” — and immunohistochemical positivity for α-smooth muscle actin, vimentin, and desmin supported smooth muscle differentiation: “Histology showed a malignant spindle cell neoplasm ... with positive immunostaining for α-smooth muscle actin, vimentin, and desmin.”\"\n5. \"Confirmed high-grade leiomyosarcoma based on smooth muscle immunophenotype and malignant spindle cells — “Pathological interpretation was a malignant spindle cell neoplasm consistent with high-grade LMS.”\"\n\n### Predicted Diagnostic Reasons (PDR):\n- Evidence statement A: \"The patient's presentation with a rapidly enlarging parietal mass, normal laboratory studies, and imaging findings of a vascular tumor with parietal bone invasion, along with histology showing a malignant spindle cell neoplasm, are consistent with meningeal hemangiopericytoma.\"\n- Evidence statement B: \"Although the clinical diagnosis was osteoblastic meningioma, the histological findings of a malignant spindle cell neoplasm with positive immunostaining for α-smooth muscle actin, vimentin, and desmin are not typical for meningioma, making this a less likely diagnosis.\"\n\n### Matching Analysis:\n\n#### GDR1: Considered meningioma (clinical diagnosis of osteoblastic meningioma)\n- Potential match with PDR statement B: \"Although the clinical diagnosis was osteoblastic meningioma...\" directly references the same clinical diagnosis.\n  - Pros: Both mention \"osteoblastic meningioma\" as a clinical diagnosis. GDR1 provides additional reasons (dural-based, bone invasion, vascular supply), while PDR statement B does not list those reasons. However, the core fact (the clinical diagnosis was made) is present in both.\n  - Cons: PDR statement B does not include the specific justifying features. But the instruction allows partial matches (e.g., \"fever\" matches \"fever due to infection\").\n- Potential match with PDR statement A: No mention of meningioma.\n- Decision: Match with statement B.\n\n#### GDR2: Considered primary lymphoma\n- No PDR statement mentions lymphoma or lymphoid proliferation. Neither statement A nor B contains any reference to lymphoma.\n- Decision: No match.\n\n#### GDR3: Considered metastasis (negative staging scans)\n- No PDR statement mentions metastasis or staging CT/PET scans.\n- Decision: No match.\n\n#### GDR4: Considered sarcoma (spindle cell morphology, IHC positive for α-smooth muscle actin, vimentin, desmin)\n- Potential match with PDR statement B: \"the histological findings of a malignant spindle cell neoplasm with positive immunostaining for α-smooth muscle actin, vimentin, and desmin\" exactly matches the quoted part of GDR4.\n  - Pros: The histological and IHC findings are identical. GDR4 also includes the phrase \"Considered sarcoma\" but the core justification (the observations) is the same.\n  - Cons: PDR statement B does not explicitly say \"sarcoma\" or \"leiomyosarcoma\"; it uses these findings to argue against meningioma. However, the factual observation is equivalent.\n- Potential match with PDR statement A: \"histology showing a malignant spindle cell neoplasm\" matches the spindle cell morphology part, but lacks the IHC. Since GDR4's justification includes both morphology and IHC, statement A is a partial match but less complete.\n- Decision: Match with statement B (the more complete match). Optionally, could also match with statement A, but the IHC is a key component. I will include only statement B as it contains the full set of findings.\n\n#### GDR5: Confirmed high-grade leiomyosarcoma (LMS)\n- No PDR statement mentions \"leiomyosarcoma\", \"high-grade LMS\", or \"smooth muscle immunophenotype\" as a final diagnosis. Both statements A and B refer to \"malignant spindle cell neoplasm\" but do not assign the specific diagnosis of LMS.\n- Decision: No match.\n\n### Summary of Matching Decisions:\n- GDR1 matches PDR statement B.\n- GDR2 matches no PDR statement.\n- GDR3 matches no PDR statement.\n- GDR4 matches PDR statement B.\n- GDR5 matches no PDR statement.\n\n### Final JSON Output:\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [\"Although the clinical diagnosis was osteoblastic meningioma, the histological findings of a malignant spindle cell neoplasm with positive immunostaining for α-smooth muscle actin, vimentin, and desmin are not typical for meningioma, making this a less likely diagnosis.\"],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [],\n    \"4\": [\"Although the clinical diagnosis was osteoblastic meningioma, the histological findings of a malignant spindle cell neoplasm with positive immunostaining for α-smooth muscle actin, vimentin, and desmin are not typical for meningioma, making this a less likely diagnosis.\"],\n    \"5\": []\n  }\n}\n```",
    "prompt_id": "mcr.reasoning_recall"
