  "708e9accb786efd1385d04b695baf146887834ce7a5128abf17163a069857fb5": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\n**Groundtruth Diagnostic Reasons (GDR):**\n1. Channelopathies were initially considered — “The differential diagnosis included channelopathies, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, and metabolic abnormalities.”\n2. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy was excluded because “The transthoracic echocardiogram (TTE) revealed ... normal wall thickness and no valvular abnormalities.”\n3. Metabolic abnormalities were excluded as “Her chemistry panel and electrolytes were normal.”\n4. Long QT syndrome was excluded given “Initial electrocardiogram ... no findings of QT prolongation …”\n5. Ischemic heart disease was excluded because ECG showed “no findings of ... infarct …”\n6. Pre-excitation syndromes were excluded since ECG showed “no findings of ... pre-excitation …”\n7. Brugada syndrome was excluded as ECG showed “no findings of ... Brugada syndrome.”\n8. Digoxin toxicity or aconitine poisoning were considered mimics — “Digoxin toxicity or aconitine poisoning may mimic ECG findings.”\n9. CPVT became the leading diagnosis after ECG evolved — “Twenty-four hours later, her ECG evolved into bidirectional ventricular tachycardia ... This raised the suspicion for CPVT …”\n\n**Predicted Diagnostic Reasons (PDR):**\n- Statement A: \"Diagnosis: Long QT Syndrome\\nEvidence: The patient's witnessed collapse, ventricular fibrillation, and QTc of 380 ms on the initial electrocardiogram suggest a channelopathy, with Long QT Syndrome being a likely cause.\"\n- Statement B: \"Diagnosis: Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy\\nEvidence: Although the transthoracic echocardiography demonstrated normal wall thickness, the patient's episode of ventricular fibrillation and reduced ejection fraction (45-50%) could still be consistent with Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy, especially if there was a missed diagnosis or variant form.\"\n\n**Matching Analysis:**\n\n**GDR1:** \"Channelopathies were initially considered\" – PDR Statement A says \"suggest a channelopathy\". Both indicate that channelopathy was part of the differential. Pros: Direct mention of channelopathy. Cons: None. Match: Statement A.\n\n**GDR2:** \"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy was excluded because normal wall thickness\" – PDR Statement B acknowledges normal wall thickness but argues it could still be HCM, not exclusion. No match.\n\n**GDR3:** \"Metabolic abnormalities excluded\" – No mention in PDR. No match.\n\n**GDR4:** \"Long QT syndrome excluded because no QT prolongation\" – PDR Statement A says QTc 380 ms suggests LQT, which contradicts exclusion. No match.\n\n**GDR5:** \"Ischemic heart disease excluded\" – No mention. No match.\n\n**GDR6:** \"Pre-excitation excluded\" – No mention. No match.\n\n**GDR7:** \"Brugada excluded\" – No mention. No match.\n\n**GDR8:** \"Digoxin/aconitine mimics\" – No mention. No match.\n\n**GDR9:** \"CPVT leading after ECG evolved\" – No mention of CPVT or bidirectional VT. No match.\n\n**Summary:** Only GDR1 has a matching statement (Statement A). All others have no matches.\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [\n      \"Diagnosis: Long QT Syndrome\\nEvidence: The patient's witnessed collapse, ventricular fibrillation, and QTc of 380 ms on the initial electrocardiogram suggest a channelopathy, with Long QT Syndrome being a likely cause.\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [],\n    \"4\": [],\n    \"5\": [],\n    \"6\": [],\n    \"7\": [],\n    \"8\": [],\n    \"9\": []\n  }\n}\n```",
