    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\n1. List all statements from Groundtruth Diagnostic Reasons and Predicted Diagnostic Reasons.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. \"Viral hepatitis was considered and excluded — “Serological examinations ruled out acute viral hepatitis with negative levels of HBsAg, anti-HBc IgM, anti-HCV, anti-HAV IgM, anti-HEV, HEV-RNA, and HCV RNA.”\"\n2. \"Autoimmune hepatitis was considered and excluded — “examined in terms of autoimmune hepatitis (ANA, AMA, ASMA, and LKM-1) but all tests were normal.”\"\n3. \"Metabolic liver diseases were considered and excluded — “examined in terms of metabolic liver diseases (transferrin saturation, ferritin, alpha-1 antitrypsin, ceruloplasmin, 24-hour urinary Cu2+, and Kayser-Fleischer ring in eye examination) but all tests were normal.”\"\n4. \"Obstructive cholestatic disease was considered and excluded — “Ultrasonography showed normal liver, gall bladder, common bile duct, and intrahepatic bile ducts… MRCP revealed a normal result.”\"\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\nA. \"The patient developed jaundice, loss of appetite, and malaise after starting Alfuzosin, with significantly elevated liver enzymes and bilirubin levels, suggesting a liver injury caused by the medication.\"\nB. \"Although serologic tests for common hepatitis viruses were negative, the possibility of a rare or atypical viral hepatitis cannot be entirely ruled out, given the patient's presentation with jaundice and elevated liver enzymes.\"\n\n2. For each statement in Groundtruth Diagnostic Reasons, consider potential matches from Predicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n\n- Groundtruth 1 (viral hepatitis excluded):\n  Potential match: Predicted B mentions negative serologic tests for common hepatitis viruses. However, Predicted B does not state that viral hepatitis was excluded; instead it says the possibility of a rare or atypical viral hepatitis cannot be ruled out. The groundtruth explicitly states that acute viral hepatitis was ruled out (including negative tests for common viruses, HEV, etc.). The predicted statement is not equivalent because it leaves the diagnosis open. Also, groundtruth 1 includes specific tests (HBsAg, anti-HBc IgM, etc.) that are not mentioned in Predicted B. No match.\n  Pros: Both mention negative serologic tests. Cons: Different conclusion (excluded vs. not ruled out), different scope (common vs. common+rare, specific tests missing). Therefore, no match.\n\n- Groundtruth 2 (autoimmune hepatitis excluded):\n  Potential match: None of the predicted statements mention autoimmune hepatitis, ANA, AMA, etc. No match.\n\n- Groundtruth 3 (metabolic liver diseases excluded):\n  Potential match: None of the predicted statements mention metabolic liver diseases, transferrin saturation, ferritin, etc. No match.\n\n- Groundtruth 4 (obstructive cholestatic disease excluded):\n  Potential match: None of the predicted statements mention ultrasonography, MRCP, or bile ducts. No match.\n\n3. Summary: No matching statements found. Each groundtruth statement has zero equivalent statements in the predicted diagnostic reasons.\n\n4. JSON output: Only include the statements that are in the Predicted Diagnostic Reasons, verbatim. Since no matches, all arrays are empty.\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [],\n    \"4\": []\n  }\n}\n```",
