  "43509d45749b931aa8d858e62d937c3ec8733303785bbf3c7454b4027719fdb5": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\n1. Groundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n   - Statement 1: \"Cranial nerve palsy — “This scan would also rule out other possible causes such as nerve palsy and schwannoma.”\"\n   - Statement 2: \"Schwannoma — “This scan would also rule out other possible causes such as nerve palsy and schwannoma.”\"\n   - Statement 3: \"Thyroid eye disease — “The globes were found to be unremarkable with no measured proptosis.”\"\n   - Statement 4: \"Orbital mass — “Noted is a right orbital linear soft tissue lesion … with enhancement pattern and signal intensity characteristics following extraocular muscle on all sequences.”\"\n   - Statement 5: \"Intracranial mass lesion — “There were no suprasellar or parasellar mass lesions.”\"\n   - Statement 6: \"Congenital syndromes (Duane syndrome, Brown’s syndrome, CFEOM) — “Clinical manifestation of an accessory extraocular muscle frequently occurs in childhood, along with numerous other congenital causes, such as Duane syndrome, Brown’s syndrome, and CFEOM.”\"\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n   - Statement A: \"The patient has a history of vertical misalignment and an elevation deficit of the right eye, which could be indicative of a problem with the superior oblique muscle.\"\n   - Statement B: \"The presence of a right orbital linear soft-tissue lesion medial to the medial rectus muscle, with characteristics similar to extraocular muscle, could be related to an abnormality in the superior oblique tendon, which is a feature of Brown syndrome.\"\n\n2. For each Groundtruth statement, evaluate potential matches:\n\n   - Groundtruth 1 (Cranial nerve palsy):\n     * Potential match with Predicted A: Mentions superior oblique muscle, which is innervated by cranial nerve IV, but does not explicitly state \"nerve palsy\" or \"cranial nerve\". The groundtruth is about ruling out nerve palsy, while predicted A is about a muscle problem. No direct match.\n     * Potential match with Predicted B: No mention of nerve palsy. No match.\n     Decision: No match.\n\n   - Groundtruth 2 (Schwannoma):\n     * No mention of schwannoma in either predicted statement. No match.\n\n   - Groundtruth 3 (Thyroid eye disease):\n     * Predicted statements do not mention thyroid eye disease or proptosis. No match.\n\n   - Groundtruth 4 (Orbital mass):\n     * Predicted B: \"right orbital linear soft-tissue lesion medial to the medial rectus muscle, with characteristics similar to extraocular muscle\" directly corresponds to the groundtruth description of \"right orbital linear soft tissue lesion … with enhancement pattern and signal intensity characteristics following extraocular muscle\". Both describe the same lesion. Strong match.\n     * Predicted A: No mention of orbital lesion. No match.\n     Decision: Match with Predicted B.\n\n   - Groundtruth 5 (Intracranial mass lesion):\n     * Predicted statements do not mention intracranial masses. No match.\n\n   - Groundtruth 6 (Congenital syndromes: Duane, Brown, CFEOM):\n     * Predicted B: ends with \"which is a feature of Brown syndrome\". This directly references one of the syndromes listed in the groundtruth (Brown's syndrome). The groundtruth includes Brown syndrome as a congenital cause. Match.\n     * Predicted A: No mention of congenital syndromes. No match.\n     Decision: Match with Predicted B.\n\n3. Summary of matching decisions:\n   - Groundtruth 1: No match.\n   - Groundtruth 2: No match.\n   - Groundtruth 3: No match.\n   - Groundtruth 4: Match with Predicted B.\n   - Groundtruth 5: No match.\n   - Groundtruth 6: Match with Predicted B.\n\n4. JSON output: Only include statements from Predicted Diagnostic Reasons, verbatim.\n\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [],\n    \"4\": [\"The presence of a right orbital linear soft-tissue lesion medial to the medial rectus muscle, with characteristics similar to extraocular muscle, could be related to an abnormality in the superior oblique tendon, which is a feature of Brown syndrome.\"],\n    \"5\": [],\n    \"6\": [\"The presence of a right orbital linear soft-tissue lesion medial to the medial rectus muscle, with characteristics similar to extraocular muscle, could be related to an abnormality in the superior oblique tendon, which is a feature of Brown syndrome.\"]\n  }\n}",
    "prompt_id": "mcr.reasoning_recall"
  },
