    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\n**Groundtruth Diagnostic Reasons (4 statements):**\n1. Pilonidal disease was considered given the location and erythema, but it was excluded because “there was no evidence of fluctuance or purulence, and no pilonidal pits were visible.”\n2. Abscess was suspected initially, but aspiration was nondiagnostic as “no fluid or purulence was obtained.”\n3. Inclusion cyst was included in the differential, but the lesion proved to be a solid, multiloculated mass rather than a simple cyst — “a complex and septated 6 × 4 × 4 cm^3 mass was excised.”\n4. Other soft-tissue tumors were considered, but immunohistochemical analysis supported a glial neoplasm and excluded epithelial or other mesenchymal tumors: “The neoplastic cells were strongly positive for GFAP, CD-99, and CD-56…They were negative for cytokeratin (AE1/AE3) which confirmed the morphologic diagnosis of ependymoma.”\n\n**Predicted Diagnostic Reasons (2 evidence statements):**\n- Evidence for Pilonidal cyst: \"The patient presented with a swelling near the gluteal cleft, which is a common location for pilonidal cysts. The absence of fevers, chills, and other symptoms, along with the presence of a complex and septated mass, supports this diagnosis.\"\n- Evidence for Epidermoid cyst: \"The patient's symptoms and the presence of a complex and septated mass are also consistent with an epidermoid cyst, which is a type of inclusion cyst.\"\n\n**Matching Analysis for Each Groundtruth Statement:**\n\n**Groundtruth 1:** States that pilonidal disease was excluded due to lack of fluctuance, purulence, and pits. The predicted evidence for pilonidal cyst claims that the location and absence of fevers/chills, plus a complex mass, support pilonidal cyst. \n- Pros: Both mention location (gluteal cleft) and absence of certain features (but different features: groundtruth mentions absence of fluctuance/purulence/pits; predicted mentions absence of fevers/chills). \n- Cons: Groundtruth explicitly excludes the diagnosis; predicted supports it. The justifications are not equivalent. No match.\n\n**Groundtruth 2:** Describes abscess suspicion and nondiagnostic aspiration. Predicted evidence does not mention abscess or aspiration at all. No match.\n\n**Groundtruth 3:** States that inclusion cyst was considered but excluded because the mass was solid and multiloculated (complex/septated) rather than a simple cyst. Predicted evidence for epidermoid cyst (type of inclusion cyst) says that the presence of a complex and septated mass is consistent with an epidermoid cyst. \n- Pros: Both mention the finding of a complex and septated mass. \n- Cons: Groundtruth uses this finding to exclude inclusion cyst; predicted uses it to include inclusion cyst. The reasoning is opposite and not equivalent. No match.\n\n**Groundtruth 4:** Describes immunohistochemical results (GFAP+, cytokeratin-) confirming ependymoma. Predicted evidence does not mention any immunohistochemistry, GFAP, or ependymoma. No match.\n\n**Summary of Final Matching Decisions:** No statements in the predicted diagnostic reasons match any of the groundtruth diagnostic reasons. All potential matches are either contradictory or unrelated.\n\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [],\n    \"4\": []\n  }\n}\n```",
